Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0253 -- MIDI stream steadiness: per-second
 MIDI work, gaps and the longest one

WHY: Gate S8/S16/S32 judge music by counters (late_writes=0 on the ISR
replay), and a counter of lateness is only as good as the stream it ran
on. The S8 fixture as first defined (Trilby's Notes, room 3 via --startr)
plays no MIDI at all, and nothing in the manifest said so except a
cumulative events=0; a song that ends 20 s into a 2-minute cell would pass
the same bar for 100 s of silence with events > 0. Whether a fixture has a
steady MIDI stream across its measured window has to be a number in every
run, not a listen.

WHAT: dos_midi_stream_sample(), once per game tick from
dos_audio_mixer_pump(): per 1 s window of uclock, the MIDI work that
reached the chip -- in isr mode the register writes the SB IRQ replayed
(SDL_DOSMidiDeferStats), in poll mode the events dispatched; whole seconds
with no sample (a stall) are empty windows. At quit, dos_midi_stream_emit()
writes one "DOS-PORT midistream" line to RUNMANI.LOG beside the ags 0250
journey report: basis, windows, the first window with any MIDI, the span
from there to the end, how many of those windows had none, the longest and
the trailing gap in seconds, and per-window min/p5/p50/max. Cost: one
uclock() per tick and a compare; 3.6 KB of .bss for 30 minutes of windows.

